=head2 SDL_UpdateHapticEffect

  my $result = SDL_UpdateHapticEffect($haptic, $effect, $data);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_UpdateHapticEffect(SDL_Haptic * haptic, SDL_HapticEffectID effect, const SDL_HapticEffect * data) >>

Update the properties of an effect.

Can be used dynamically, although behavior when dynamically changing direction may be strange. Specifically the effect may re-upload itself and start playing from the start. You also cannot change the type either when running SDL_UpdateHapticEffect().

=over

=item haptic - SDL3::Haptic object or undef. the SDL_Haptic device that has the effect.

=item effect - integer. the identifier of the effect to update.

=item data - SDL3::HapticEffect object or undef. an SDL_HapticEffect structure containing the new effect properties to use.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RunHapticEffect

  my $result = SDL_RunHapticEffect($haptic, $effect, $iterations);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RunHapticEffect(SDL_Haptic * haptic, SDL_HapticEffectID effect, Uint32 iterations) >>

Run the haptic effect on its associated haptic device.

To repeat the effect over and over indefinitely, set `iterations` to `SDL_HAPTIC_INFINITY`. (Repeats the envelope - attack and fade.) To make one instance of the effect last indefinitely (so the effect does not fade), set the effect's `length` in its structure/union to `SDL_HAPTIC_INFINITY` instead.

=over

=item haptic - SDL3::Haptic object or undef. the SDL_Haptic device to run the effect on.

=item effect - integer. the ID of the haptic effect to run.

=item iterations - unsigned integer. the number of iterations to run the effect; use `SDL_HAPTIC_INFINITY` to repeat forever.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

